19:52:31 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It does happen, but it is much more noticeable in **TV shows** than in major movies. Big theatrical films often clear music more broadly from the start—especially since studios expect theatrical, home video, TV, international, and streaming distribution. If a famous song is central to a movie’s identity, the studio is also more likely to pay to preserve it. Older TV, documentaries, low-budget films, and independent productions are where replacements are common. Their original music licenses may have covered only broadcast or a limited home-video term, because streaming and later formats were not anticipated or were too expensive to include. When the show is reissued, clearing dozens or hundreds of songs can be impractical. Well-known examples include: - **WKRP in Cincinnati**: Many original rock songs were replaced in syndication and some home releases because the music rights had not been cleared for those uses. - **Dawson’s Creek**: The original theme, Paula Cole’s “I Don’t Want to Wait,” was replaced with “Run Like Mad” on some DVD and streaming versions for years, though some later releases restored it. - **Beverly Hills, 90210**: Numerous songs were replaced on streaming and DVD editions. - **The Wonder Years**: Music licensing made it difficult to release complete versions for a long time; early DVD releases had substantial substitutions. - **Miami Vice**: Some releases have had music substitutions, though later editions restored much of the original soundtrack. For films, it tends to show up in more obscure cases, older low-budget titles, documentaries, or movies with a lot of licensed background music. Sometimes the change is only in a scene’s radio music or nightclub track, so even viewers familiar with the film may not notice. So your intuition is right: it is not routine for prominent studio movies to lose their signature songs on streaming. But music-rights limitations are real and are a major archival and re-release problem—particularly for television.
