19:52:44 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Not necessarily, but for a typical major studio movie, it is often *effectively* locked in for practical purposes. When a studio licenses music for a feature film, the agreement may cover broad rights such as: - theatrical exhibition - home video and digital sales - television - streaming/on-demand - international distribution - future media “now known or later devised” - use for the life of copyright, or sometimes in perpetuity So for many modern studio films, especially ones with major songs, the music is cleared broadly enough that it stays intact across later releases. But it is still a contract question, not an automatic rule. A film can have narrower or time-limited licenses, particularly if it is: - older, from before home video or streaming was anticipated - independent or low-budget - documentary-heavy - made for a limited theatrical or festival run - packed with incidental music from radios, bars, performances, etc. - using music under a deal that later expires or excludes a new type of distribution Also, the studio may own the film but not the song or recording. It needs permission from both the song publisher and the owner of the specific recording, and those permissions can have conditions or expiration dates. So: for a big modern feature, viewers can usually assume the soundtrack will remain the same. But legally it is not inherently permanent; it remains intact because the original licenses were negotiated broadly enough, or because the studio later pays to renew or restore them.
